    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Explore the Excel workspace, the Ribbon tabs, Quick Access Toolbar, Formula Bar, and Status Bar.",
    "content": "# Lesson 01: Introduction to Excel & The Ribbon Interface\n\nExcel is the world's most used tool for organising, calculating and analysing data. Sales reports, budgets, stock lists, attendance sheets - almost every job uses it. This first lesson gives you a confident tour of the Excel window so nothing on the screen feels strange.\n\n## 1. The parts of the Excel window\n\n| Part | Where | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ribbon** | Top of the window | All commands, grouped in tabs |\n| **Tabs** | Home, Insert, Page Layout, Formulas, Data, Review, View | Each tab is a set of related tools |\n| **Quick Access Toolbar** | Very top left | Save, Undo, Redo - always visible |\n| **Name Box** | Left, above column A | Shows the address of the selected cell (e.g. `B3`) |\n| **Formula Bar** | Right of the Name Box | Shows the real content or formula of the cell |\n| **Grid** | Middle | Columns (A, B, C...), rows (1, 2, 3...), cells |\n| **Sheet tabs** | Bottom | Sheet1, Sheet2... |\n| **Status Bar** | Very bottom | Sum, Average, Count of selected cells; zoom |\n\n## 2. The most important tabs\n- **Home** - fonts, colours, number formats, alignment, copy/paste, sort & filter. You will use it 80% of the time.\n- **Insert** - tables, charts, PivotTables, pictures.\n- **Formulas** - the function library and named ranges.\n- **Data** - sort, filter, remove duplicates, data validation, Power Query.\n- **View** - freeze panes, zoom, gridlines.\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: The Formula Bar shows the truth\nType `=10*5` in cell **A1** and press **Enter**. The cell shows **50**, but click A1 again and the **Formula Bar** shows `=10*5`. The cell shows the *result*; the formula bar shows *how* it was calculated.\n\n### Example 2: The Status Bar quick calculator\nType 100, 200 and 300 in A1:A3. Select the three cells and look at the bottom right: **Average: 200   Count: 3   Sum: 600**. No formula needed. Right-click the Status Bar to add **Min** and **Max**.\n\n### Example 3: Collapse the Ribbon for more space\nPress **Ctrl + F1** to hide/show the ribbon. Double-clicking any tab name does the same.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Press the **Alt** key once - letters appear on every tab and button. Press **H** for Home, then the shown letter for any button. You can operate the whole ribbon from the keyboard.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Looking at the cell instead of the Formula Bar** to understand a value.\n- **Hiding the ribbon accidentally** - press **Ctrl + F1**.\n- **Not noticing the Status Bar** - it saves you many quick formulas.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Open a new blank workbook.\n2. In A1:A5 type: 120, 340, 90, 410, 75.\n3. Without writing any formula, find the sum, average, minimum and maximum.\n4. In B1 type a formula that multiplies A1 by 2. Check what the cell shows and what the Formula Bar shows.\n5. Use **Alt** shortcuts to make cell A1 bold.\n\n:::solution\n3. Select **A1:A5**. The Status Bar shows **Sum = 1035**, **Average = 207**. Right-click the Status Bar and tick **Minimum** and **Maximum**: **Min = 75**, **Max = 410**.\n4. In **B1** type `=A1*2` and press **Enter**. The cell shows **240**; the Formula Bar shows `=A1*2`.\n5. Click **A1**, then press **Alt**, **H**, **1** (the Bold button). A1 becomes bold. (Of course, **Ctrl + B** does the same.)\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- The ribbon groups commands into tabs; Home is used most.\n- The Formula Bar shows what is really in a cell.\n- The Status Bar gives instant Sum, Average and Count.\n",
