    "title": "Lesson 02: Understanding Workbooks, Worksheets, Rows, Columns, and Cells",
    "slug": "lesson-02-understanding-workbooks,-worksheets,-rows,-columns,-and-cells",
    "category": "excel",
    "category_name": "Excel & Spreadsheets",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Learn how Excel structures data across workbooks, individual sheets, rows, columns, and grid cells.",
    "content": "# Lesson 02: Understanding Workbooks, Worksheets, Rows, Columns, and Cells\n\nBefore building any report, you need the vocabulary of Excel: **workbook**, **worksheet**, **row**, **column**, **cell** and **range**. These words appear in every instruction, formula and error message.\n\n## 1. The building blocks\n\n| Term | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Workbook** | The whole Excel file | `Sales 2026.xlsx` |\n| **Worksheet (sheet)** | One page/tab inside the workbook | `January`, `February` |\n| **Column** | Vertical line of cells, named by letters | Column **C** |\n| **Row** | Horizontal line of cells, named by numbers | Row **5** |\n| **Cell** | Where a row and column meet | **C5** |\n| **Range** | A rectangle of cells | **A1:D10** |\n\nA modern worksheet has **16,384 columns** (A to XFD) and **1,048,576 rows**.\n\n## 2. Reading cell and range addresses\n- `B3` - column B, row 3.\n- `A1:A10` - the 10 cells in column A from row 1 to 10.\n- `A1:C3` - 9 cells (3 columns x 3 rows).\n- `A:A` - the entire column A.\n- `3:3` - the entire row 3.\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: A simple data layout\n\n| | A | B | C |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **1** | Product | Units | Price |\n| **2** | Eye Drops A | 120 | 25 |\n| **3** | Eye Drops B | 80 | 32 |\n\n- The value **80** is in cell **B3**.\n- The prices are in range **C2:C3**.\n- The whole table is **A1:C3**.\n\n### Example 2: Selecting quickly\n- Click the column letter **B** to select the whole column.\n- Click a row number to select the whole row.\n- Click the triangle at the top-left corner (or **Ctrl + A**) to select the whole sheet.\n- Type `A1:C3` into the **Name Box** and press **Enter** to select that range.\n\n### Example 3: One workbook, many sheets\nKeep each month on its own sheet (`Jan`, `Feb`, `Mar`) inside one workbook `Sales 2026.xlsx`, and a `Summary` sheet that adds them up (Lesson 35).\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Always organise data as a **list**: one header row, one record per row, one type of information per column. Every advanced feature (sort, filter, PivotTables) depends on this.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Saying \"sheet\" when you mean \"workbook\"** - the file vs a tab inside it.\n- **Leaving empty rows inside a table** - Excel thinks the table ends there.\n- **Putting two pieces of information in one cell** (e.g. `120 units`).\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Recreate the table in Example 1 and add two more products.\n2. Write down the address of: the header \"Price\", the units of the last product, and the range of all prices.\n3. Select the range of all units using the Name Box.\n4. How many cells are in the range `B2:E6`?\n\n:::solution\n1. Example data:\n\n| | A | B | C |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Product | Units | Price |\n| 2 | Eye Drops A | 120 | 25 |\n| 3 | Eye Drops B | 80 | 32 |\n| 4 | Gel C | 60 | 40 |\n| 5 | Wipes D | 150 | 18 |\n\n2. Header \"Price\" = **C1**; units of last product = **B5**; all prices = **C2:C5**.\n3. Click the Name Box, type `B2:B5`, press **Enter**.\n4. B to E = 4 columns; rows 2 to 6 = 5 rows; **4 x 5 = 20 cells**.\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Workbook = file; worksheet = tab; cell = column letter + row number.\n- A range is written `TopLeft:BottomRight`, like `A1:C10`.\n- Keep data as a clean list with one header row.\n",
