    "title": "Lesson 04: Entering and Editing Data: Text, Numbers, and Dates",
    "slug": "lesson-04-entering-and-editing-data-text,-numbers,-and-dates",
    "category": "excel",
    "category_name": "Excel & Spreadsheets",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Understand how Excel automatically interprets different data types and how to edit cells without overwriting.",
    "content": "# Lesson 04: Entering and Editing Data: Text, Numbers, and Dates\n\nEvery spreadsheet starts with typing data. Excel treats **text**, **numbers** and **dates** differently - and mistakes here cause wrong totals, broken sorting and formulas that do not work.\n\n## 1. Three kinds of data\n- **Text** (labels): aligned **left** by default. Example: `Riyadh`, `Product A`.\n- **Numbers**: aligned **right**. Example: `250`, `12.5`.\n- **Dates**: also numbers (Lesson 59), aligned **right**. Example: `17/11/2026`.\n\nIf a number is aligned to the **left**, Excel probably sees it as **text** - and will not add it.\n\n## 2. Entering and editing\n- Type and press **Enter** (down) or **Tab** (right).\n- **F2** edits the cell content in place (cursor at the end).\n- **Double-click** a cell to edit it.\n- **Esc** cancels the typing.\n- **Delete** clears the cell content.\n- **Alt + Enter** starts a new line *inside* the cell.\n\n## 3. Quick entry tricks\n- **Ctrl + ;** inserts today's date; **Ctrl + Shift + ;** inserts the current time.\n- Select a range, type a value, press **Ctrl + Enter** - the value fills **all** selected cells.\n- **AutoComplete**: type the first letters of a value already in the column, press Enter to accept.\n- **Alt + Down** shows a list of values already in the column (pick-from-list).\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Leading zeros disappear\nTyping `00512` gives **512**. To keep zeros (IDs, phone numbers): type an apostrophe first: `'00512`, or format the column as **Text** before typing.\n\n### Example 2: Fill 50 cells with \"Pending\"\nSelect **C2:C51**, type `Pending`, press **Ctrl + Enter**. All 50 cells now contain *Pending*.\n\n### Example 3: Long numbers become 1.23E+15\nA 16-digit card or ID number shows as scientific notation and the last digits become 0. Enter such IDs as **text** (apostrophe or Text format) - they are identifiers, not numbers to calculate.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Never type units inside number cells (`25 SAR`, `10 kg`). Put the unit in the header: `Price (SAR)`. Then you can calculate with the numbers.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Numbers stored as text** (green triangle in the corner) - click the warning icon > **Convert to Number**.\n- **Typing dates in an inconsistent format** - some become text.\n- **Pressing Enter in the formula bar** expecting a new line - use **Alt + Enter**.\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\nCreate this table quickly:\n\n| Order ID | Date | Customer | Qty | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 00101 | today | Al Nahdi | 25 | Pending |\n| 00102 | today | Al Dawaa | 40 | Pending |\n| 00103 | today | United | 15 | Pending |\n\n1. Keep the leading zeros in Order ID.\n2. Insert today's date with a shortcut.\n3. Fill all three \"Pending\" cells in one action.\n4. Add a second line inside the customer cell `Al Nahdi` showing `Jeddah Branch`.\n\n:::solution\n1. Type `'00101`, `'00102`, `'00103` (or select A2:A4, **Home > Number Format > Text**, then type). They stay left-aligned with zeros.\n2. Click **B2** > **Ctrl + ;** > **Enter**. Repeat for B3:B4 (or select B2:B4, press **Ctrl + ;** then **Ctrl + Enter**).\n3. Select **E2:E4**, type `Pending`, press **Ctrl + Enter**.\n4. Double-click **C2**, place the cursor after `Al Nahdi`, press **Alt + Enter**, type `Jeddah Branch`, press **Enter**. (Wrap Text turns on automatically.)\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Text aligns left, numbers and dates align right by default.\n- **F2** edits, **Ctrl + Enter** fills a selection, **Ctrl + ;** inserts today.\n- Store IDs with leading zeros as text; keep units out of number cells.\n",
