    "title": "Lesson 05: Understanding Cell Referencing: Columns and Rows",
    "slug": "lesson-05-understanding-cell-referencing-columns-and-rows",
    "category": "excel",
    "category_name": "Excel & Spreadsheets",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Master cell addresses, continuous ranges, non-adjacent ranges, and entire row/column references.",
    "content": "# Lesson 05: Understanding Cell Referencing: Columns and Rows\n\nFormulas work by **referring to cells**. When you write `=B2*C2`, Excel looks up the values in those cells. If the values change, the result updates instantly. This is what makes spreadsheets powerful.\n\n## 1. Referring to cells in a formula\n- Start every formula with `=`.\n- Type the cell address, or **click** the cell while writing the formula.\n- Example: `=B2+B3` adds the values in B2 and B3.\n\n## 2. Why use references instead of numbers?\n\n| Formula | Problem / benefit |\n|---|---|\n| `=120*25` | Fixed result - if units change, you must edit the formula |\n| `=B2*C2` | **Updates automatically** when B2 or C2 changes |\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Calculating a line total\n\n| | A | B | C | D |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Product | Units | Price | Total |\n| 2 | Eye Drops A | 120 | 25 | `=B2*C2` -> **3,000** |\n| 3 | Eye Drops B | 80 | 32 | `=B3*C3` -> **2,560** |\n\nChange B2 to 150 - D2 instantly becomes **3,750**.\n\n### Example 2: Copying the formula down\nWrite `=B2*C2` in D2, then drag the **fill handle** (small square at the bottom right of the cell) down to D3. Excel automatically changes the formula to `=B3*C3`. This is called a **relative reference** (Lesson 31).\n\n### Example 3: Seeing which cells a formula uses\nClick D2 and press **F2** - the referenced cells are highlighted in colour. Or use **Formulas > Trace Precedents** to draw arrows.\n\n### Example 4: Show all formulas\nPress **Ctrl + `** (the key above Tab) to show formulas instead of results in the whole sheet. Press again to return.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Build formulas by **clicking** cells instead of typing addresses - it prevents typing mistakes like `=B2*C3`.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Forgetting the `=`** - Excel shows the text `B2*C2`.\n- **Typing numbers inside formulas** that should come from cells.\n- **Referencing the wrong row** after inserting or moving data.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\nCreate this sheet:\n\n| | A | B | C | D | E |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Product | Units | Price | Total | Total + VAT |\n| 2 | Lubricating Gel | 60 | 40 | | |\n| 3 | Eye Wipes | 150 | 18 | | |\n| 4 | Drops Mini | 200 | 12 | | |\n\n1. In D2 calculate Units x Price and copy it down.\n2. In E2 calculate the total including 15% VAT using D2, and copy it down.\n3. Change the price of Eye Wipes to 20 and observe the updates.\n\n:::solution\n1. **D2**: `=B2*C2` -> 2,400. Drag the fill handle to D4: D3 `=B3*C3` -> **2,700**, D4 `=B4*C4` -> **2,400**.\n2. **E2**: `=D2*1.15` -> **2,760**. Fill down: E3 -> **3,105**, E4 -> **2,760**.\n3. Change **C3** to 20: D3 becomes **3,000** and E3 becomes **3,450** automatically.\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Formulas start with `=` and refer to cells like `B2`.\n- References update automatically when the data changes.\n- Copying a formula down adjusts the row numbers.\n",
