    "title": "Lesson 06: Basic Formatting: Fonts, Borders, Colors, and Alignment",
    "slug": "lesson-06-basic-formatting-fonts,-borders,-colors,-and-alignment",
    "category": "excel",
    "category_name": "Excel & Spreadsheets",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Transform raw, ugly numbers into clean, professional, and visually compelling tables.",
    "content": "# Lesson 06: Basic Formatting: Fonts, Borders, Colors, and Alignment\n\nFormatting makes data easy to read. A well-formatted report helps your manager understand it in seconds - and looks professional. Formatting changes **how** data looks, not its value.\n\n## 1. Font formatting (Home tab)\n\n| Tool | Shortcut |\n|---|---|\n| Bold / Italic / Underline | **Ctrl + B / I / U** |\n| Font and size | Home > Font group |\n| Font colour | Home > Font Color (A with a bar) |\n| Fill colour (background) | Home > Fill Color (paint bucket) |\n| Format Cells dialog | **Ctrl + 1** |\n\n## 2. Borders\n**Home > Borders** dropdown: *All Borders*, *Outside Borders*, *Thick Box Border*, *Bottom Double Border* (classic for totals). In **Ctrl + 1 > Border** you can choose line style and colour.\n\n## 3. Alignment\n- Left / Center / Right (horizontal).\n- Top / Middle / Bottom (vertical).\n- **Wrap Text** - long text in several lines (Lesson 8).\n- **Center Across Selection** (Ctrl + 1 > Alignment) - centres a title over several columns **without merging**.\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: A professional header row\nSelect A1:E1 > **Bold** > Fill colour dark blue > Font colour white > Center > Bottom border. Now the header stands out.\n\n### Example 2: Totals row\nSelect the total row > **Bold** > *Top and Double Bottom Border*. This is the accounting standard look.\n\n### Example 3: Format Painter\nFormat one header, double-click the **Format Painter** (paintbrush), click other headers on other sheets, press **Esc**. Same format everywhere in seconds.\n\n### Example 4: Cell Styles\n**Home > Cell Styles** offers ready designs: *Heading 1*, *Title*, *Total*, *Good/Bad/Neutral*. Quick and consistent.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Avoid **Merge & Center** inside data tables - merged cells break sorting, filtering and copying. Use **Center Across Selection** for titles instead.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Too many colours** - use 1-2 accent colours.\n- **Merged cells** inside tables.\n- **Formatting entire columns** with fills and borders - makes the file larger and Ctrl + End wrong.\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\nUse your table from Lesson 5 and:\n1. Format the header: bold, white text on dark blue fill, centred.\n2. Add *All Borders* to the table.\n3. Add a total row with SUM of the Total column, bold with a double bottom border.\n4. Put the title `November Sales` in A1 above the table, centred across A:E without merging.\n\n:::solution\n1. Insert a new row 1 for the title first (right-click row 1 > *Insert*) so the header moves to row 2. Select **A2:E2** > **Ctrl + B** > Fill: dark blue > Font colour: white > **Center**.\n2. Select **A2:E5** > **Home > Borders > All Borders**.\n3. In **A6** type `Total`; in **D6** `=SUM(D3:D5)` and **E6** `=SUM(E3:E5)`. Select **A6:E6** > **Ctrl + B** > **Borders > Top and Double Bottom Border**.\n4. Type `November Sales` in **A1** > select **A1:E1** > **Ctrl + 1 > Alignment > Horizontal: Center Across Selection** > OK. The title is centred but A1:E1 are not merged.\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Ctrl + B/I/U, fill, font colour and borders make data readable.\n- Ctrl + 1 opens all formatting options.\n- Use Center Across Selection instead of merging cells.\n",
