    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Ensure numbers display their true meaning with currency symbols, decimal precision, and standard date formats.",
    "content": "# Lesson 07: Number Formatting: Currency, Percentages, Decimals, and Dates\n\nNumber formats control how a number is **displayed** - currency, percentage, decimals, dates - without changing the actual value. A cell can contain `0.15` but display **15%**.\n\n## 1. Common number formats\n\n| Format | Shortcut | `1234.567` displays as |\n|---|---|---|\n| General | **Ctrl + Shift + ~** | 1234.567 |\n| Number (2 decimals, separator) | **Ctrl + Shift + !** | 1,234.57 |\n| Currency | **Ctrl + Shift + $** | $1,234.57 (or your currency) |\n| Percentage | **Ctrl + Shift + %** | 123457% |\n| Short date | **Ctrl + Shift + #** | (a date) |\n| Increase / decrease decimals | Home > `.00` buttons | 1,234.6 / 1,234.567 |\n\n## 2. Currency vs Accounting\n- **Currency**: symbol right next to the number.\n- **Accounting**: symbol aligned at the left edge of the cell, zeros shown as `-`. Common in financial reports.\n- For Saudi Riyal: **Ctrl + 1 > Number > Currency > Symbol: SAR** (or the ﷼ symbol).\n\n## 3. Custom formats (Ctrl + 1 > Custom)\n- `#,##0 \"units\"` -> 1,250 units (still a number!)\n- `0.0%` -> 15.3%\n- `#,##0,\"K\"` -> 1,250,000 shows as 1,250K\n- `dd-mmm-yyyy` -> 17-Nov-2026\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Percentages\nGrowth = (This month - Last month) / Last month. With 11,500 and 10,000: `=(B2-A2)/A2` = **0.15**. Press **Ctrl + Shift + %** -> **15%**.\n\n### Example 2: Display vs real value\nA cell displays **1,234.57** (2 decimals) but the real value is 1234.567. Formulas use **1234.567**. To actually round the value, use `ROUND` (Lesson 29).\n\n### Example 3: Units without text\nApply custom format `#,##0 \"pcs\"`. Cell shows *1,250 pcs* but SUM still works because the value is the number 1250.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Type `15%` directly into a cell - Excel stores 0.15 and formats it as a percentage automatically.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Typing 15 and formatting as %** -> shows **1500%**. Type `15%` or `0.15`.\n- **Thinking formatting rounds the value** - it only changes display.\n- **Mixing formats** in one column (some with 2 decimals, some with none).\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\n\n| | A | B | C | D |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Region | Last Month | This Month | Growth |\n| 2 | Jeddah | 10000 | 11500 | |\n| 3 | Makkah | 8000 | 7600 | |\n| 4 | Madinah | 6000 | 6900 | |\n\n1. Format B2:C4 as SAR currency with no decimals.\n2. Calculate growth in D2:D4 and format as percentage with 1 decimal.\n3. Give C2:C4 a custom format that shows thousands as `K`.\n\n:::solution\n1. Select **B2:C4** > **Ctrl + 1 > Number > Currency** > Decimal places **0**, Symbol **SAR** (or ﷼) > OK. Shows e.g. *SAR 10,000*.\n2. **D2**: `=(C2-B2)/B2` > fill down. Select D2:D4 > **Ctrl + Shift + %** > **Increase Decimal** once. Results: Jeddah **15.0%**, Makkah **-5.0%**, Madinah **15.0%**.\n3. Select **C2:C4** > **Ctrl + 1 > Custom** > type `#,##0.0,\"K\"` > OK. 11500 shows as **11.5K**, 7600 as **7.6K**. The values are unchanged.\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Number formats change display, not the value.\n- Use Ctrl + Shift + $ / % / ! for quick currency, percent and number formats.\n- Custom formats can add units or K/M without turning numbers into text.\n",
    "tags": "Excel, Lesson 07, Fundamentals, Beginner",
    "read_time": 4,
    "author": "Mohammed Bargisy",
